From 436f22b90077fb293f2aa7257bfa11c3f5b61436 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Lamb <paul@paulscode.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 14:34:09 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 12/14] wallet: a successful restamp is unusual, not broken

log_broken prints **BROKEN** in the log. An operator reading that after
doing exactly what they were told to do has every reason to think
something went wrong, and Core Lightning's own test framework treats any
**BROKEN** in a node's log as a failed run, which is how this surfaced:
the two canned-database tests started passing their migration and then
errored in teardown.

The message is worth keeping and worth being loud about, since the change
is one way. log_unusual is the level for that. The two refusals above it
keep log_broken, because each precedes a deliberate exit.
---
 wallet/wallet.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/wallet/wallet.c b/wallet/wallet.c
index 41e22b8ba..74a26e6ac 100644
--- a/wallet/wallet.c
+++ b/wallet/wallet.c
@@ -4892,7 +4892,14 @@ bool wallet_sanity_check(struct wallet *w)
 							     &chainparams->genesis_blockhash));
 				return false;
 			}
-			log_broken(w->log, "Wallet was stamped with block 0 "
+			/* Noteworthy, not broken: the operator asked for
+			 * this and it worked.  log_broken would print
+			 * **BROKEN**, which alarms anyone reading the log
+			 * and which Core Lightning's own test framework
+			 * treats as a failed run. The two refusals above
+			 * keep log_broken, because they precede a
+			 * deliberate exit. */
+			log_unusual(w->log, "Wallet was stamped with block 0 "
 					   "(%s); adopting this chain's "
 					   "chain_hash %s",
 				   fmt_bitcoin_blkid(tmpctx, &chainhash),
-- 
